* Palantir says neither it nor Washington can switch off NATO's Maven: "There are no kill switches" [palantir military autonomous-weapons] {SL:ai-weapons-targeting}
* Seventeen former national security officials urge the D.C. Circuit to rehear Anthropic's Pentagon designation case [anthropic pentagon us-federal-policy autonomous-weapons] {SL:anthropic-pentagon-split}
* Google's multi-agent personal health agent published in Nature after more than 7,000 expert and user annotations [healthcare agents evals google-deepmind]
* Nine-modality machine learning failed to beat a few clinical risk factors at predicting depression relapse in 564 patients [healthcare evals]
* Neural-ODE model nominated three rescue targets for mutant B cells and mouse experiments confirmed all three [ai-for-science drug-discovery]
* Judge hears cross-motions on fair use in music publishers' lyrics case against Anthropic [copyright anthropic us-federal-policy]
* Revised DHS AI directive lets the chief AI officer waive any requirement, including its prohibited uses [us-federal-policy surveillance privacy]
* OpenAI proposes a five-stage disclosure process for AI-found bugs and says such findings will "increase sharply" [openai cyber-defense us-federal-policy] {SL:ai-enabled-hacking}
