  "date": "2026-10-26",
  "text": [
    "As of 26 October the Autonomous Warfare Command has named leadership and an implementation deadline. A letter from the leaders of Project Agincourt, released on 21 October, sets out a four-star Autonomous Warfare Command that the project is tasked with making operational by 1 October 2027, with the director of the Defense Innovation Unit as chief executive officer and a Navy SEAL senior chief as chief operating officer. The letter's stated test is financial rather than doctrinal: \"Every auditable dollar we spend should reduce cost-per-kill or risk-to-force.\" It argues that \"Low-cost drones have changed the character, speed and economics of war\". A senior Department of War official said the command \"is here to stay\". The letter sets no standard for human control of target selection, and the memorandum itself could not be read: it returns HTTP 403 to both of our fetchers.",
    "The named programme at the centre of the arc has not been shown to be fielded. Army Cyber Command told The Intercept that ECHOTRIBBLE is \"primarily focused\" on offensive missions, and described neutralising a target as \"disabling the threat completely or temporarily, deleting the threat from the target\". It also said a governance board decides when its agents may work unsupervised. ARCYBER did not answer The Intercept's questions, and no published policy governs autonomous target selection by the programme. On the deployed side, Marine Corps commands were ordered to issue Maven Smart System accounts at every echelon, with licensing effective 1 October; the message itself is not published and the Pentagon, Palantir and the Marine Corps did not answer questions about it.",
    "The week's one statement about control came from a vendor rather than a government. Palantir's executive vice-president for the UK and Europe said of NATO's Maven deployment: \"I can provide categorical reassurance that where customers have bought our software, they are in full operational control.\" \"There are no kill switches.\" Euronews ties those remarks to reporting on Starlink disruptions in Ukraine, not to the new US command or to the Marine Corps expansion, and no source connects the two. Both halves of the arc's question are therefore still open: no AI-built targeting system has been shown in operational use, and no government has published rules for autonomous target selection."
  ],
  "changed": "The command moved from an announced intention to a dated implementation project with named executives and a stated metric of cost-per-kill. What did not move is control: the letter sets no human-control standard, ECHOTRIBBLE's governance rests on an unpublished board, and the only assurance of operational control published this week came from a contractor speaking about a NATO system, with no source linking it to the new command."
},
"anthropic-pentagon-split": {
  "date": "2026-10-26",
  "text": [
    "As of 26 October the designation is being contested by people who used to run the agencies that made it, and nothing new has been published about what replaced Claude. On 20 October 17 bipartisan former national security officials, among them a former Director of National Intelligence and two former National Security Advisers, filed a 26-page brief supporting Anthropic's petition for rehearing en banc in the D.C. Circuit. They argue that \"Left undisturbed that ruling will threaten all defense contractors\", that \"No other court can revisit it\", and that the panel decision \"would set a dangerously erroneous standard\".",
    "The brief goes at the process rather than the merits of the two capabilities at issue. It argues that had the department \"engaged in a lawful process, it would have considered the specific risks posed by the two capabilities Anthropic refused to provide\", which it names as mass domestic surveillance and lethal autonomous weapons, and that the department \"has not explained why DOD should have the first, much less how it could exercise it lawfully. Nor has DOD reconciled the second with the United States' commitment that [its military use of AI be in compliance with applicable international laws]\". It describes the designation as applied before any administrative steps or formal justification, alongside public denunciations.",
    "The second half of the arc's question is where it was in September. No department document has been published saying what replaced Claude in which role, and none says what replaces the contract safeguards Anthropic declined to relax. The Pentagon did not comment on the brief, and the court has not ruled on the petition. The Project Agincourt letter setting a date for an Autonomous Warfare Command, released the day after the brief was filed, does not mention Anthropic, the designation or any vendor dispute, and no source connects the two in either direction."
  ],
  "changed": "The challenge gained institutional backing: 17 former national security officials, bipartisan and including a former Director of National Intelligence, filed in support of rehearing and framed the designation as a process failure that would reach every defence contractor. Nothing was published about the replacement for Claude or the contract terms, and the Pentagon's own autonomy announcement the next day made no reference to the dispute."
}
}

