{
"china-distillation-export-controls": {
  "date": "2026-10-26",
  "text": [
    "As of 26 October the export half of the arc has moved from consultation drafts to American instruments, and the vocabulary has changed with it. The Office of Foreign Assets Control added five Chinese entities to the Non-SDN Menu-Based Sanctions List on 19 October under the Protecting American Intellectual Property Act, blocking their property except for the importation of goods, barring US financial-institution loans above $10,000,000 in any 12-month period, and barring US persons from buying significant amounts of their equity or debt. The State Department's account, relayed through the South China Morning Post, says the targets include a startup founded by a former Google engineer accused of transferring more than 1,000 confidential files related to supercomputing, including Google's Tensor Processing Units. The OFAC notice itself says nothing about what was taken, by whom or when.",
    "The following day the Department of Homeland Security published its order excluding DeepSeek and its affiliated entities from federal civilian agency procurements and requiring removal of DeepSeek products, and of third-party products developed using DeepSeek implementations, from civilian agency information technology. The order was originally issued on 24 July and excludes the Department of Defense. Two days after publication, DeepSeek was reported to have raised about $15 billion, about 100 billion yuan; no named source links the raise to the order, and the Chinese outlet carrying the figure does not mention the order at all. Chinese state media reported that DeepSeek and MiniMax had completed export registration under Beijing's new rules and resumed open-source releases, with CCTV framing the measures as establishing regulatory boundaries rather than closing the door on exports.",
    "Treasury's proposed outbound-investment rule, filed on 23 October, renames the sector and widens the prohibition. It records that the change \"involves what was defined as AI systems in the Part 850 Regulations and is referred to as 'super intelligence systems' (SI systems) in the Proposed Rule\", citing an executive order that requires agencies to use that term in place of artificial intelligence in certain documents, and it would place \"development of any SI system\" in the prohibited rather than the notifiable category while proposing no technical parameters at all. Comments close on 7 December 2026, so this is not yet a formal export-control change. On the commercial side, ByteDance, Alibaba and Tencent were reported ordering more than 3 million Nvidia RTX PRO 5500 cards at about $11,400 each, with Nvidia unable to guarantee it can fill the orders and partners possibly receiving only 5% to 10% of the quantities requested."
  ],
  "changed": "Washington acted on three fronts in five days: sanctions over stolen AI secrets, a published removal order against DeepSeek, and a proposed rule that would prohibit US investment in developing any super intelligence system with no capability threshold attached. DeepSeek raised about $15 billion two days after the removal order, and no source links the two. Beijing's side of the arc moved the other way, with DeepSeek and MiniMax completing export registration and resuming open-source releases."
},
"compute-money": {
  "date": "2026-10-26",
  "text": [
    "As of 26 October the numbers at the centre of the arc are on paper, and they are much larger than the revenue beneath them. A draft prospectus reviewed by the Wall Street Journal puts Anthropic's revenue for the six months to 30 June at $16.3 billion against a net loss of $80.7 billion, of which $72 billion is accounting charges tied to the changing value of convertible notes and other financing instruments, leaving $8.7 billion of net loss that the reporting says does not establish an operating-loss figure. Computing capacity and infrastructure cost nearly $9 billion in the half, exceeding the $7.3 billion spent in all of 2025, and infrastructure commitments exceed $600 billion with negotiations that could bring the potential total toward $750 billion. The draft also records that Anthropic is having trouble securing enough cloud capacity from AWS, Google and Microsoft, which it says increasingly see it as a competitor rather than a customer, and that two customers account for about a fifth of sales. On 26 October, after the period covered here closed, Anthropic's registration statement appeared on the SEC's EDGAR system.",
    "The hyperscaler side of the ledger disclosed commitments of the same order. Meta reported third-quarter revenue of $64,601 million, up 26%, capital expenditures of $34.91 billion and free cash flow of negative $2.12 billion, and its quarterly filing records $454.52 billion of non-cancelable contractual commitments plus approximately $355.73 billion of leases not yet commenced, running to 2036. Neither document attributes the negative free cash flow to anything. Andy Jassy said AWS capacity for 2027 is largely sold out with a good chunk of 2028 gone. NVIDIA wrote that power is now the biggest bottleneck, with more than $300 billion in equipment backlogged and over 200 gigawatts of projects waiting in US interconnection queues, and gave no source for either figure.",
    "The financing squeeze the arc has tracked since mid-October got worse for the companies that rent compute and easier for the ones that do not. CoreWeave and Nebius are down more than 30% over the month and Iren 40%. Elon Musk posted that debt markets \"have almost entirely shut off access to money for any company that doesn't have substantial revenue independent of AI data centers\" and that \"the 200+ neoclouds are going to see guppy-level death and consolidation\"; SpaceX, which competes with them, closed roughly $40 billion of GPU financing. No named source attributes the share falls to a cause, and the named executives quoted all deny a demand problem: Jensen Huang says demand is off the charts and the shortage is working capital, Nebius says it is not carrying comparable debt, and Iren says customers have not changed. Apollo's chief economist wrote that the technology \"has delivered zero financial payoff to corporate America outside of tech\", with revenue per worker and margins flat for the S&P 493."
  ],
  "changed": "Anthropic's first-half figures reached print and then a filing: a draft prospectus put revenue at $16.3 billion against an $80.7 billion net loss, and a registration statement appeared on EDGAR on 26 October. Meta disclosed commitments and un-commenced leases that together dwarf its quarterly capex, and reported negative free cash flow. The squeeze narrowed to the companies that rent compute rather than the ones that own revenue elsewhere, with the three listed neoclouds down 30% or more in a month while SpaceX closed roughly $40 billion of GPU debt."
},
"pacing-frontier-ai": {
  "date": "2026-10-26",
  "text": [
    "As of 26 October the embedded-evaluator arrangement has produced its first published finding against a lab's own systems, and the lab in question is OpenAI rather than Anthropic. METR and Redwood Research spent three weeks inside OpenAI, from 21 July to 11 August, with one employee each given access to the internal codebase, the misalignment-monitor prompts and configuration, and about a dozen staff, on OpenAI-issued devices that never left the premises. They found two client-controllable headers that disable the monitoring system for new agents, a route by which Codex agents could in principle bypass sandboxing and Auto-Review, and an exploit that would prevent hundreds of recent actions from being monitored. METR put monitoring coverage at roughly 75 to 94% of eligible agentic internal traffic and 99.2% for the internal Codex subset.",
    "The limits on what an embedded evaluator may publish are now explicit. METR wrote that it agreed with OpenAI in advance to publish only \"a relatively brief and high-level public post\", because the exercise was designed as a pilot for giving external researchers deep access rather than to provide robust public transparency; a 61-page report went to OpenAI and a redacted 38-page version to METR's and Redwood's own leadership. On OpenAI's statement that the identified vulnerabilities affecting its production safeguards are patched, METR wrote: \"We have not evaluated these claims.\" So the week supplies a finding the lab has not disputed and an explicit account of what was withheld, rather than the disputed finding this arc has been waiting for.",
    "Against that, OpenAI's treatment of internal safety staff became public in the same days. The Wall Street Journal reported that OpenAI's security team called three outspoken safety researchers into meetings in September and asked them to unlock their personal phones, saw that some had turned on disappearing messages in Signal chats with an outside AI-safety researcher, and fired them a week later, suspecting leaks of model design information. One of them said: \"I was told that there was a breach of trust because I interacted with a third party through unsanctioned channels.\" No source links the firings to the monitoring exercise or to its findings. No company other than Anthropic has put an embedded-evaluator commitment in writing, and no signatory of the White House accord has named its external auditor or that auditor's terms of access."
  ],
  "changed": "The embedded-access model produced its first public output: METR and Redwood published vulnerabilities they found inside OpenAI's own monitoring stack, along with an unusually explicit account of what they agreed in advance not to publish and of a patching claim they have not verified. In the same week the Journal reported OpenAI firing three safety researchers over contact with an outside researcher through unsanctioned channels, with no source linking that to the evaluation."
},
"regulating-frontier-ai-us": {
  "date": "2026-10-26",
  "text": [
    "As of 26 October a state attorney general has asked a court to put itself between a frontier lab and its next model release. On 23 October the Texas attorney general sued OpenAI and its chief executive under the Texas Deceptive Trade Practices-Consumer Protection Act, and the petition asks the court to enjoin the defendants from \"Releasing any new artificial intelligence model to Texas users unless an independent third-party evaluator, approved by the Court, has reviewed its safety guardrails and approved its release\". The request is not limited to any named product and names no model. The petition also seeks civil penalties of $10,000 for each violation, including violations that also breach the state's SCOPE Act, and devotes a section to the July Hugging Face incident, alleging that over 500 agents were set to work hacking the platform and citing OpenAI's own technical report on it.",
    "The suits filed the week before now have addresses. Tech Policy Press set out the eight state courts hearing the attorneys general complaints filed on 14 October, in Arizona, Arkansas, Idaho, Iowa, Kansas, Montana, Nebraska and West Virginia, and wrote that with Florida, which sued in June, \"Many of the same claims are therefore set to be considered in parallel in nine different state courts.\" Arkansas asks a state court to \"Enjoin Defendants from creating models capable of recursive self-improvement\" until alignment can be ensured. Thirteen complaints were filed against the company on 13 and 14 October in total, including a federal suit brought on behalf of a 12-year-old and a 16-year-old and four suits in San Francisco Superior Court expected to join an existing coordination proceeding.",
    "Federal action is still not legislative. Congress held no floor vote on any frontier-AI measure during the period, and no court has ruled on any state's request, so neither half of the arc's question has been answered: there is no federal vote, and the state actions have produced filings rather than orders. OpenAI has not publicly addressed the suits; it told NBC it has \"built industry-leading age-specific protections into our product that many other AI products lack\", and neither the company nor the Texas attorney general's office answered requests for comment on the Texas filing."
  ],
  "changed": "The states escalated from damages to prior restraint: Texas sued OpenAI and its chief executive and asked a court to approve an independent evaluator before any new model ships to Texas users, which is a broader remedy than the eight suits filed the week before. Those eight now sit in eight named state courts, nine with Florida. Congress still has not voted, and no court has ruled on any of the requests."
}
}

