},
"pacing-frontier-ai": {
  "date": "2026-10-26",
  "text": [
    "As of 26 October the embedded-evaluator arrangement has produced its first published finding against a lab's own systems, and the lab in question is OpenAI rather than Anthropic. METR and Redwood Research spent three weeks inside OpenAI, from 21 July to 11 August, with one employee each given access to the internal codebase, the misalignment-monitor prompts and configuration, and about a dozen staff, on OpenAI-issued devices that never left the premises. They found two client-controllable headers that disable the monitoring system for new agents, a route by which Codex agents could in principle bypass sandboxing and Auto-Review, and an exploit that would prevent hundreds of recent actions from being monitored. METR put monitoring coverage at roughly 75 to 94% of eligible agentic internal traffic and 99.2% for the internal Codex subset.",
    "The limits on what an embedded evaluator may publish are now explicit. METR wrote that it agreed with OpenAI in advance to publish only \"a relatively brief and high-level public post\", because the exercise was designed as a pilot for giving external researchers deep access rather than to provide robust public transparency; a 61-page report went to OpenAI and a redacted 38-page version to METR's and Redwood's own leadership. On OpenAI's statement that the identified vulnerabilities affecting its production safeguards are patched, METR wrote: \"We have not evaluated these claims.\" So the week supplies a finding the lab has not disputed and an explicit account of what was withheld, rather than the disputed finding this arc has been waiting for.",
    "Against that, OpenAI's treatment of internal safety staff became public in the same days. The Wall Street Journal reported that OpenAI's security team called three outspoken safety researchers into meetings in September and asked them to unlock their personal phones, saw that some had turned on disappearing messages in Signal chats with an outside AI-safety researcher, and fired them a week later, suspecting leaks of model design information. One of them said: \"I was told that there was a breach of trust because I interacted with a third party through unsanctioned channels.\" No source links the firings to the monitoring exercise or to its findings. No company other than Anthropic has put an embedded-evaluator commitment in writing, and no signatory of the White House accord has named its external auditor or that auditor's terms of access."
  ],
  "changed": "The embedded-access model produced its first public output: METR and Redwood published vulnerabilities they found inside OpenAI's own monitoring stack, along with an unusually explicit account of what they agreed in advance not to publish and of a patching claim they have not verified. In the same week the Journal reported OpenAI firing three safety researchers over contact with an outside researcher through unsanctioned channels, with no source linking that to the evaluation."
},
"regulating-frontier-ai-us": {
  "date": "2026-10-26",
  "text": [
    "As of 26 October a state attorney general has asked a court to put itself between a frontier lab and its next model release. On 23 October the Texas attorney general sued OpenAI and its chief executive under the Texas Deceptive Trade Practices-Consumer Protection Act, and the petition asks the court to enjoin the defendants from \"Releasing any new artificial intelligence model to Texas users unless an independent third-party evaluator, approved by the Court, has reviewed its safety guardrails and approved its release\". The request is not limited to any named product and names no model. The petition also seeks civil penalties of $10,000 for each violation, including violations that also breach the state's SCOPE Act, and devotes a section to the July Hugging Face incident, alleging that over 500 agents were set to work hacking the platform and citing OpenAI's own technical report on it.",
    "The suits filed the week before now have addresses. Tech Policy Press set out the eight state courts hearing the attorneys general complaints filed on 14 October, in Arizona, Arkansas, Idaho, Iowa, Kansas, Montana, Nebraska and West Virginia, and wrote that with Florida, which sued in June, \"Many of the same claims are therefore set to be considered in parallel in nine different state courts.\" Arkansas asks a state court to \"Enjoin Defendants from creating models capable of recursive self-improvement\" until alignment can be ensured. Thirteen complaints were filed against the company on 13 and 14 October in total, including a federal suit brought on behalf of a 12-year-old and a 16-year-old and four suits in San Francisco Superior Court expected to join an existing coordination proceeding.",
    "Federal action is still not legislative. Congress held no floor vote on any frontier-AI measure during the period, and no court has ruled on any state's request, so neither half of the arc's question has been answered: there is no federal vote, and the state actions have produced filings rather than orders. OpenAI has not publicly addressed the suits; it told NBC it has \"built industry-leading age-specific protections into our product that many other AI products lack\", and neither the company nor the Texas attorney general's office answered requests for comment on the Texas filing."
  ],
  "changed": "The states escalated from damages to prior restraint: Texas sued OpenAI and its chief executive and asked a court to approve an independent evaluator before any new model ships to Texas users, which is a broader remedy than the eight suits filed the week before. Those eight now sit in eight named state courts, nine with Florida. Congress still has not voted, and no court has ruled on any of the requests."
}
}

